1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,669
This is Keel, by team FinLink.

2
00:00:02,719 --> 00:00:05,924
Keel is a company wallet run by an AI agent.

3
00:00:05,974 --> 00:00:10,880
It pays an international purchase order only when the proof exists.

4
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:19,807
Pacifico Foods, a food importer in Chile, buys forty eight thousand dollars of agar from Lingnan, a supplier in Hong Kong.

5
00:00:19,857 --> 00:00:21,123
They have never met.

6
00:00:21,173 --> 00:00:22,308
So who pays first?

7
00:00:22,358 --> 00:00:25,532
If Pacifico pays first, the goods may never ship.

8
00:00:25,582 --> 00:00:28,953
If it pays later, the supplier will not ship at all.

9
00:00:29,003 --> 00:00:32,769
And one day an email arrives: we changed our bank account.

10
00:00:32,819 --> 00:00:33,690
It looks real.

11
00:00:33,740 --> 00:00:34,480
It is fraud.

12
00:00:35,430 --> 00:00:39,278
So we built Keel: a company wallet, run by an AI agent.

13
00:00:39,328 --> 00:00:41,476
The importer keeps its own key.

14
00:00:41,526 --> 00:00:45,374
The agent acts only inside the limits the company sets.

15
00:00:45,424 --> 00:00:49,910
And the money waits in a smart contract, until the proof exists.

16
00:00:50,860 --> 00:01:00,292
What you see next is the real app, talking to our API on AWS, with real transactions on Base Sepolia, a public test network.

17
00:01:00,342 --> 00:01:02,510
The order is one test dollar.

18
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:06,108
Pacifico Foods signs up as an importer.

19
00:01:06,158 --> 00:01:10,880
The balance on screen comes from the company's Safe wallet, on chain.

20
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:16,157
The importer starts a new order and uploads the supplier's proforma.

21
00:01:16,207 --> 00:01:24,009
On AWS, our quarantined AI reads it and returns typed fields only: the amount, the deadline, the incoterm and the HS code.

22
00:01:24,059 --> 00:01:26,262
It never returns the payee account.

23
00:01:26,312 --> 00:01:33,407
Thirty percent goes as an advance, seventy percent when the shipment is proven, and Harbor Line is the carrier.

24
00:01:33,457 --> 00:01:35,080
The order goes to Lingnan.

25
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:39,081
Lingnan, the supplier, opens the same order.

26
00:01:39,131 --> 00:01:41,760
It sees exactly where it will be paid.

27
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:47,539
That account is part of what both companies sign, so no email can change it later.

28
00:01:47,589 --> 00:01:53,460
Lingnan signs with its own wallet: a signature over structured data, not over a PDF.

29
00:01:54,410 --> 00:01:56,088
The importer signs last.

30
00:01:56,138 --> 00:01:59,185
It signs the terms; it does not send money.

31
00:01:59,235 --> 00:02:01,490
From here, the agent takes over.

32
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:10,913
Within a minute, the agent on AWS checks the company's policy and deposits the dollar into the escrow, through the funding gate.

33
00:02:10,963 --> 00:02:16,107
In the same transaction, the contract pays the thirty cent advance to Lingnan.

34
00:02:16,157 --> 00:02:19,570
Every step leaves a hash on a public block explorer.

35
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:27,339
Lingnan ships the goods and uploads the bill of lading and the invoice, each upload signed by its wallet.

36
00:02:27,389 --> 00:02:35,320
The AI reads them, and the rules engine compares them with the signed order: the order number, the amount and the HS code.

37
00:02:36,270 --> 00:02:44,188
Harbor Line, the carrier, signs that the cargo is on board: a second, independent signature, and the agent submits the shipment.

38
00:02:44,238 --> 00:02:49,854
The documents match the order, so the importer approves the balance with its own signature.

39
00:02:49,904 --> 00:02:53,713
Without it, the verifier releases it after the dispute window.

40
00:02:53,763 --> 00:02:58,320
The contract pays the seventy cents to Lingnan, and the order is complete.

41
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:01,290
And if the supplier never ships?

42
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:05,365
The deadline passes, and the balance goes back to the importer.

43
00:03:05,415 --> 00:03:09,570
Anyone can trigger the refund, so the importer depends on no one.

44
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,344
What if the AI is tricked?

45
00:03:12,394 --> 00:03:13,641
The AI only reads.

46
00:03:13,691 --> 00:03:15,011
The engine decides.

47
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:16,236
People authorize.

48
00:03:16,286 --> 00:03:17,822
The contract executes.

49
00:03:17,872 --> 00:03:24,020
A fake account email changes nothing, because the account comes from the signed order.

50
00:03:24,970 --> 00:03:33,641
And if someone steals the agent's key and tries to pay an account the company never approved, the funding gate rejects it on chain.

51
00:03:33,691 --> 00:03:36,437
Here is that rejection, with its own hash.

52
00:03:36,487 --> 00:03:37,502
The AI can fail.

53
00:03:37,552 --> 00:03:39,100
The money does not move.

54
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:49,047
The agent also helps the importer buy better: trends, supplier dependence, and the cash coming up, fitted to the size of the company.

55
00:03:49,097 --> 00:03:54,217
It sees only numbers, with suppliers as S1 and S2, never documents or names.

56
00:03:54,267 --> 00:03:57,006
It cannot sign, and it cannot move money.

57
00:03:57,056 --> 00:04:01,020
Next, predictive models trained on each company's own data.

58
00:04:01,970 --> 00:04:06,158
Two companies signed once, and the money waited for the proof.

59
00:04:06,208 --> 00:04:10,055
Seven attacks rejected on chain, each with a public hash.

60
00:04:10,105 --> 00:04:13,610
Two hundred ninety four contract tests, all passing.

61
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:15,798
And zero owner keys on a server.

62
00:04:15,848 --> 00:04:16,686
This is Keel.

63
00:04:16,736 --> 00:04:17,370
Thank you.
